{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled from 1155 to 1194 a significant figure in Crusader history. He inherited the County of Tripoli from his father Raymond II a position he successfully defended against numerous enemies. His reign was marked by constant warfare political maneuvering and efforts to consolidate his power within the fragile Crusader states.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Countess Sophia of Calabria. He received a thorough education and was groomed for leadership from a young age. Upon his father's death in 1155 Raymond ascended to the County facing immediate challenges from Muslim forces and internal dissent. He quickly established himself as a capable ruler demonstrating military prowess and diplomatic skill.\n\nRaymond III’s reign involved complex alliances and conflicts. He frequently allied with other Crusader rulers particularly those of Antioch and Jerusalem to counter the power of the Zengid dynasty a powerful Muslim dynasty controlling much of Syria. He engaged in numerous battles and sieges successfully defending Tripoli from attacks by Nur ad-Din and his successors. His military campaigns extended into Palestinian territories and he frequently raided Muslim lands to weaken his enemies and secure resources.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Diplomacy* A key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his skillful diplomacy. He understood the importance of maintaining relationships with both Christian and Muslim powers. He negotiated truces and alliances when necessary and used diplomacy to achieve his strategic goals. He was known for his pragmatic approach often prioritizing the security of Tripoli over ideological purity. His military campaigns were not always successful but they served to maintain a degree of stability and deter larger-scale invasions. He also invested in strengthening Tripoli's defenses building fortifications and maintaining a well-equipped army.\n\nRaymond III’s reign also saw significant economic development in Tripoli. The city flourished as a major trading center connecting Europe with the East. He encouraged trade and invested in infrastructure contributing to the city's prosperity. However his rule was not without its problems. He faced internal rebellions and struggled to maintain control over the various factions within the county.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1194 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. He is remembered as one of the most effective and long-reigning Counts of Tripoli. He successfully defended the county against numerous enemies maintained its economic prosperity and played a key role in shaping the political landscape of the Crusader states. His legacy is one of resilience diplomacy and military strength a testament to his ability to navigate the turbulent world of the Middle Ages. He is often depicted as a shrewd and pragmatic ruler who prioritized the survival and prosperity of his county above all else.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2558029294013977}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -1.0921385288238525}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -3.58813522325363e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.4318398573086597e-05}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -4.529942543740617e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.15126226842403412}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.08380527794361115}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.00014590153296012431}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.6689160474925302e-05}, {"token": "9", "logprob": -1.3828182090946939e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.014750940725207329}, {"token": "▁a", 